+## Confirmed findings            ← consumed by super-design, one task per finding
+- [Should-fix] Section 1 Storage ("A row exists only for a key set explicitly; absence means the registry default"; "`nudge_threshold` is a registry key whose backing store is the existing `seats.nudge_threshold` column") vs Commands (`get` "with `(default)` when unset"; `unset KEY` "delete the row") — `unset` and the `(default)` marker are undefined for the column-backed `nudge_threshold`. The column is NOT NULL DEFAULT 0, never has a `seat_config` row, and cannot tell an explicit 0 from unset.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✗ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: ground seat: spec lines 77-85, 111 and 121 define row absence, `(default)` and "delete the row"; a search for "backing", "unset", "explicit" and "(default)" found no definition for column-backed keys; line 221 lists "default vs explicit" and "nudge_threshold backing" as test areas without defining either. Schema: migrations/node/0001_node_baseline.sql:438 `nudge_threshold INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` (cited by the finding; no seat opened the file). The reproduce seat's REJECT concedes the spec defines neither verb for this key and asks for a one-line clarification. Seat severities split Nit / Should-fix; Should-fix kept because this is one of only two initial keys and both verbs are specified for it.
+  fix-shape hint: state that `unset` on a column-backed key writes the registry default (and resets the `wake_on` mirror) and that `get` prints `(default)` when the value equals the default; or give column-backed keys a marker row.
+- [Should-fix] Section 1 Storage (`seat_config` columns `writer_provenance`, `writer_binding`) vs Commands (`ht human config seat set [--seat S]` any key, any named local seat, "recorded as an operator decision with provenance `operator:local-user:<uid>`"); Trust policy section — a human-path write to the column-backed `nudge_threshold`, including on another seat, has nowhere to record provenance, and the A5 exception (threshold writes limited to the seat's own top-level binding) is not stated.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗ REJECT, outvoted 2-1 / ground ✓)
+  evidence: reproduce seat: the only provenance columns are in `seat_config`; the same section sends `nudge_threshold` writes to `seats.nudge_threshold` with "no data migrates"; the Trust policy section adds "the seat-config write rules" but never gives A5 an operator exception. Ground seat: spec lines 59, 78, 118-120, 142-143 searched for provenance, audit, writer_binding, A5 and operator; no storage location and no A5 note. The refute seat's dissent says the record goes to "the existing operator-decision mechanism" but names no storage for it and concedes "the spec could say where provenance is stored for column-backed keys". Seat severities split Should-fix / Nit; Should-fix kept because AGENTS.md requires a change that adds an accepted limit or weakens an invariant to update TRUST-POLICY.md in the same commit.
+  fix-shape hint: pick one and write it down: a provenance row in `seat_config` for column-backed keys, provenance columns on `seats`, or an explicit statement that column-backed keys record none; add the A5 operator exception to the Trust policy section. One spec edit can close this and the finding above.
+- [Nit] Section 1 Registry (`nudge_threshold` integer -1..9; "an unknown key or out-of-range value is refused") vs Commands ("`nudge-threshold [N]` and `wake-on` stay as aliases … output and M12 are unchanged") — the registry's strict -1..9 refusal and the alias's existing clamp of any negative value to -1 are not reconciled, so `nudge-threshold -5` and `config seat set nudge_threshold -5` can disagree.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗ REJECT, outvoted 2-1 / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/protocol/priority.rs:133-134 "Any negative value clamps to -1; above 9 is an error"; src/cli/commands.rs:4188 parses the alias through `NudgeThreshold::parse` → `new`, so `-5` stores -1 today; spec lines 92-97 and 122 state both promises and no reconciliation. The refute seat's dissent (src/cli/nudge_threshold.rs takes an already-parsed value, so the alias normalises before the registry sees it) is the same resolution the reproduce seat names; the spec does not state it.
+  fix-shape hint: one sentence: the alias clamps client-side before the registry check and `config seat set` refuses values below -1, or the registry clamps too.
