+## Rejected (with reason)        ← so re-roasts don't re-litigate
+- Section 1 Commands / Trust policy — gating human-tier writes on the `ht human` namespace contradicts TRUST-POLICY A1 ("confers no canonical authority") and A1 is not amended.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). TRUST-POLICY.md:269 says operator decisions "attribute no person and are not refused in an agent session", so `operator:local-user:<uid>` does not claim the person acted. The spec states the accepted limit (design lines 139-146) and cites A1 at line 61. Residue: no `--operator` flag and no A1 wording note; nit-level.
+- Section 2 Selection, Arrival — owed items are treated as per binding, but pending items and ACKs belong to the seat.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. TRUST-POLICY.md (~line 1347): "a delivery row is each binding's own stream, so its owedness is decided per binding"; line 1064: read cursors remain per binding. Side hook delivery is marked "(unchanged)". Receipt-bearing rows are the pre-existing ht-327 exception.
+- Section 3 — the retry ladder's bound and exhaustion behaviour are unstated, and no accepted limit is recorded against delivery-contract rule 5.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). A displayed side chat takes the existing typed-input refusal path (src/notification/policy.rs:394); a refusal restores the prior ladder (src/scheduler/mod.rs ~92, ~774, ~1369), so nothing drops the wake. The user approved the deferral ("lgtm"). Residue: one sentence saying a refusal keeps retrying.
+- Section 2 Selection — the root-identification method has no defined input; the marker must be persisted, needing a migration and wire field the spec does not plan.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The "must be persisted" premise is one possible S1 outcome; S1 lists rollout and storage paths, which an adapter could resolve from the stored `native_session`. The spec stops the routing bead if S1 finds no marker.
+- Section 1 Commands / Trust policy — the human tier rests on a client-declared `tier` field the daemon cannot verify.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The Trust policy section states "no adversarial verification is added" and AGENTS.md forbids adding it. A crafted client is the accepted same-user bypass. The daemon check still validates key, type and range and records provenance. Residue: accepted-limit wording could say "any same-user protocol client".
+- Section 1 "Removing the node setting" / Registry — no rule for an intent already selected or armed when `programmatic_wake` flips off.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). The spec promises only "read per wake attempt"; a later flip applies to the next attempt. The existing stale-authority path ("queue wake no longer authorized", ht-c877) is left in place. Worst case is one extra queue wake or one native fallback. Residue: one sentence.
+- Section 2 Selection — a side binding's unread copy could keep the seat owed and re-wake the root repeatedly.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. `wake_owed_to_reached` (src/store/wake.rs:742, 803-810) checks unread per reached binding only immediately before submission; for a queue wake to the root, the reached set is the root. Caveat recorded by two seats: the full scheduler path from a settled attempt to a new reservation was not traced.
+- Section 3 — removing `Ask a follow-up question` assumes the string appears only in the /side composer.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. src/harness/codex/composer.rs:10-14 documents two separate Codex constants, `PLACEHOLDER` and `SIDE_PLACEHOLDER`; the ground seat fetched codex-rs/tui/src/chatwidget/constructor.rs and found `normal_placeholder_text` and `side_placeholder_text` stored separately. A future reuse would fail the existing wake e2e send cases.
+- Section 2 Selection — root and /side bindings are assumed to record an identical liveness pid, start stamp and boot; no spike verifies it.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). Same-process grouping by fingerprint already exists (src/store/bindings.rs `is_superseded_now`, `concurrent_session`; src/ports.rs ~2627, ~2725). A mismatch falls back to native, which the spec states. Residue: add a liveness-column dump to S1.
+- Section 2 Selection / Spikes S1 — in-TUI session switches (/new, /resume, /fork) may leave earlier root bindings connected, so "exactly one root" may name a stale session.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. No evidence that superseded bindings stay connected. Two apparent roots fail "exactly one" and select native. Arrival counts only root contact, so a mis-sent wake reads unreceived. Residue: add a /new-then-/side check to S1.
+- Section 2 Spikes S3, S4 / Qualification — only S1 has a stop condition; no fallback if S3 or S4 fails.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). The `side-queue/group` case requires the item delivered in the root turn and nothing typed into the side chat, so a failed S3 or S4 fails qualification. Queue wake is opt-in per seat and Section 3 is independent. Residue: one line "if S3 or S4 fail, routing stays native".
+- Section 2 Selection / Spikes S1 — the whole of Section 2 rests on an unchecked root marker.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The spec names this as open, schedules S1 to answer it, and states the stop: "If spike S1 finds no root marker, the routing bead stops with a blocker for a human scope decision". Section 3 ships regardless.
+- Section 2 Selection — the busy-sibling admission rule does not say what a refusal leads to.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). A refusal falls into the existing admission-refusal and ladder path; Section 3 then defers native while a side chat is displayed. The rule is provisional on S2. Residue: a clarifying sentence.
+- Section 2 Selection — a side binding stuck Busy or Unknown after the side chat is abandoned can block queue wake indefinitely.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. A refused queue admission selects native, and native defers only while a side composer is displayed, so the root still wakes. The rule is provisional on S2. Stale turn state is existing machinery the spec does not change.
+- Section 2 Selection — a closed side chat may never get another turn; the side binding's lifecycle is undefined.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. A lingering side binding shares the root's pid, so selection still resolves to the root; side contact never settles the wake; Section 3 works from the composer read, not binding lifecycle. Residue: record the side binding's lifecycle as an S1 observation.
+- Section 1 Trust policy / Context — the human tier depends on harness ask rules firing for the new spellings, which is unverified per harness and under bypass modes.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. src/harness/claude/permission_rules.rs:23 asks on the `[spelling, "human"]` prefix, which covers every `human` subcommand; TRUST-POLICY.md:791 and ~2997 already record the matching limits. The spec accepts a missing or bypassed rule as a limit.
+- Section 1 Commands — the human write is an operator decision "like `ht human seat rebind --operator`" but has no `--operator` flag.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). The missing daemon-side check is the accepted cooperative limit. The spec commits to a TRUST-POLICY update in the same commit, which can add a row or the flag. Residue: state the flag spelling against TRUST-POLICY.md:269.
+- Section 1 Commands / Storage — `operator:local-user:<uid>` is recorded even for an agent-issued write, and the spec does not say whether `writer_binding` is filled.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). The spec promises no after-the-fact detection of bypassed approvals; the provenance value marks an operator-path write, not a person. Residue: say that `writer_binding` records the invoking binding for human-path writes when one exists.
+- Section 1 Commands / Trust policy — a bypassed agent can write any key on every local seat through `--seat`.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. Cross-seat reach is stated in the command text ("any named local seat") and follows from the accepted limit. Both keys are bounded and reversible. Residue: accepted-limit wording could add "on any local seat".
+- Section 3 — the defer works by making the side placeholder read as composer text, so it holds only while Codex declares no composer stash.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. src/harness/codex.rs:223 declares `composer_stash: Unsupported`; src/notification/policy.rs:391-394 then returns `Skip("typed input without composer_stash")`. The stash-supported profile is Claude's. The `side-native-defer/group` case tests the outcome.
+- Section 1 "Removing the node setting" — with no per-seat default or inheritance, every new seat needs its own human-approved command.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. Per-seat, human-tier opt-in is the stated requirement (B1, B6); B10 records "Remove, no migration". Queue wake was already off by default and native wake still delivers.
+- Section 1 release tooling — the wake e2e driver must set the flag after enrollment and before the first send; no ordering is stated.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The driver owns sequencing and can only name a seat once it exists. The flag is read per wake attempt, so a write before the first send takes effect. Residue: a note for the driver bead.
+- Section 1 release tooling — release qualification no longer exercises queue wake unless `--queue-proof` is passed.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. Auto-selection only fired where an operator had enabled the node setting; the default run was already native-only. The spec adds `side-queue/group` and keeps existing queue-proof cases.
+- Section 1 Registry / "Removing the node setting" — "read per wake attempt" assumes queue infrastructure is built regardless of the node flag.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. src/app.rs ~2131-2160 builds `QueueRecovery` and `QueueWakeRuntime` whenever the state dir exists; the `(None, None)` arm keys on the state dir. The flag is read only at src/notification/queue.rs:372 and :390, the two per-attempt gates the spec moves.
+- Section 2 vs Section 3 — Section 2 is a large mechanism for a corner case Section 3 already handles.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The Goal requires that a seat with programmatic wake on and a /side chat open "still gets its idle wake in the root session"; Section 3 only defers. B8 and B10 record the user's approval.
+- Section 1 Storage — a human-tier grant survives reversible retirement and carries over to a later occupant.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The spec states it: "reversible retirement keeps them". The seat is the durable principal; the key only selects a wake transport and is reversible with `unset`.
+- Section 1 Commands / Error handling — subagent refusal is stated only for the agent path; read-only bindings and a nonexistent or retired `--seat` have no rule.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). The human path inherits the `ht human` and unified-seat-selector semantics. No stated requirement is contradicted. Residue: one sentence on the human-path bullet.
+- Section 1 "Removing the node setting" — users who enabled the node flag silently lose queue wake on upgrade.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. B10 is an answered decision; `doctor` points at the per-seat command; affected users fall back to native wake. A CHANGELOG line is release-process work under "the release docs follow".
+- Section 2 Selection — the root marker is assumed to be a positive signal; a side-only connected set could be named as root.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The spec requires the adapter to name the root from S1 evidence and says "Side sessions are never queue targets"; a set with no nameable root selects native. Nothing in the spec supports inferring root as "not a side".
+- Section 2 Selection / Arrival — no rule for two bindings claiming root or a marker inherited by a /side session.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. "Exactly one" fails for two claimants and selection falls back to native (design.md:152-157). S1, S3 and the fake-peer tests cover the marker.
+- Section 2 Selection — the busy-sibling check is a sampled read; the race and the treatment of Unknown are unstated.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). The rule is a provisional best-effort guard and S2 measures the overlap case. Existing convention treats Unknown as never idle. Residue: one sentence accepting the sampled window and stating Unknown counts as Busy.
+- Section 2 Arrival — the root-only receipt rule covers only the queued wake; native receipt is unaddressed.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). Native receipt is pre-existing and unchanged; Section 3 stops native typing while a side chat is displayed. Residue: one sentence saying native receipt is unchanged.
+- Section 2 Selection / S1 — bindings enrolled before the upgrade can never be named as root.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. The spec states the behaviour: "a root the adapter cannot name, select native as today". The lifecycle-payload-only premise is what S1 determines.
+- Registry table — the spec does not say whether a `programmatic_wake` write triggers a wake reconsideration or lifts an unreceived suppression.
+  reason: 2 REJECT / 1 CONFIRM (Nit). The stated contract is only "applies without a daemon restart", which holds at the next attempt. Residue: one sentence, probably "a write is not a wake trigger".
+- Section 1 release tooling / Trust policy — the e2e driver runs the human-tier write from a script, an indirect route the accepted limit does not name.
+  reason: 3 REJECT. Running `ht human` through an approved interpreter is an instance of the accepted bypass limit (spec lines 143-146). The driver writes only to seats in its owned instance. Residue: accepted-limit wording could name scripts.
